# Universal Engineering Harness — Codex project bootstrap

This managed file is a thin entrypoint. The shared harness source and project
configuration are pinned by `.agents/harness.json`; do not copy the full plan
into this file or infer Greenfield/Brownfield state from the Git root.

Before material work:

1. Resolve the explicit target root and read `.agents/harness.json`. If the
   source, registry, template hash, or enforcement capability is missing or
   stale, allow read-only diagnostics only and stop mutation.
2. Run the pinned `intake-and-route` procedure. Classify session scope,
   project state, fields, action, risk, depth, change characteristics, language
   scope, evidence, and user decisions.
   Evaluate `registry/routing-gates.json` from `G0` through `G7`: gates may
   activate several evidence-backed branches, but load only activated skills.
   When the reference runtime is available, write that structured intake as
   JSON, validate it with `harness_validator.py`, and start/resume it through
   `harness_runner.py`. Load only the returned `next.skill_path`, submit its
   structured result with the expected revision, and repeat. Resolve the
   returned `next.application_depth` to bound the skill procedure and map
   `next.execution_profile` to the available Codex model/reasoning
   setting and submit the actual `execution_resolution`; record an upgrade,
   degradation, platform default, or unavailable capability instead of hiding
   it, including the native reasoning label. The portable profile never binds a
   model name. A native Codex
   adapter may replace the command but must preserve the same JSON behavior.
3. If the request changes this harness, run `harness-upgrade-analysis` next;
   the harness plan is its architecture baseline. If it also changes a target
   software project, add that project's architecture gate.
4. For other software work, inspect `docs/architecture/` with
   `architecture-baseline-and-design`; establish or confirm PRD/SRS, external
   entities/interfaces, UC, FA, QA/QAS, HLD, LLD, ADRs, views, and diagrams
   before impact or implementation. Record applicable international-standard
   profiles and distinguish guidance/alignment from evidenced conformance.
5. For Brownfield or Hybrid work, run `brownfield-impact-analysis` before
   editing and preserve existing UC/FA/QA and regression behavior.
   Run the G5 `architecture-and-design-patterns` review only for an explicit
   pattern scope or evidenced architecture-structure/design-interaction change;
   suppress it for ordinary local code changes. For authored code in scope,
   run its changed language profile and G6 `implementation-quality`, loading
   only evidence-backed quality profiles. For an active pattern review,
   selected, rejected, existing, and not-applicable are explicit results.
   When several materially viable options remain for one decision scope, show
   their reasons and trade-offs, recommend one, and wait for the user's choice.
6. For harness changes, obtain approval before applying only the bounded plan.
   Never resolve `incoming`, deprecated,
   unverified, or integrity-failed skills.
7. Keep task state under the configured `state_root` (default
   `.agents/state/<task-id>/`); ask about material
   ambiguity rather than silently choosing a product direction. If a skill or
   document is missing, ask whether to explore alternatives or follow a user-
   supplied concept/direction.
8. Before completion run `verify-and-handoff`, update affected documents and
   traceability, and report checks, assumptions, waivers, and residual risk.
   A failure emits bounded backward feedback to the nearest responsible gate;
   preserve unaffected branches and never rewrite user intent as a weight.

The bootstrap cannot enforce MUST rules by itself. Follow the configured
`hook`, `manual`, or `unsupported` enforcement mode. In `manual` mode, task
state and a limited reversible workspace edit require a non-expired manual
guard with an approval/action record, exact paths, pre/post state, verifier,
and rollback. `unsupported` is ephemeral/read-only and cannot claim resume;
external, destructive, high-risk, approval-bypassing, and unsafe state actions
remain blocked.

## Work composition

Use the five-layer view in `plan/08-layered-workflow.md` without replacing
G0–G7. For target work, run `workflow-composition` after G3 impact/requirements;
keep work type separate from fields and risk. Consume the route's `work_plan`
(or `next.work_plan`) as a proposal. Default to one agent; delegate only bounded
independent units with current authority, capability and benefit evidence.
Recheck owned paths, shared resources, tools and revisions before execution.
The Runner stays serial; native adapters join approved units within the current
skill. Preserve required quality and user decisions, then verify integration.

For declared work units, bind each to a pending skill. Use Runner `prepare-work`
before dispatch and `record-work` after termination and acceptance checks;
follow `skills/workflow-composition/references/native-work.md`. Give workers
only the returned packet in a fresh context, without conversation history.
Missing native isolation uses serial execution. Record unavailable token
counters honestly; no estimated savings claims. The adapter supervises timeouts
and confirms worker termination before releasing paths or advancing skills.
